  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warm front is crossing northern New York and Vermont \nearly this morning, bringing a few additional showers to the area. \nSo far about 0.50-1.00 inches of rainfall has fallen in a swath from \nthe St. Lawrence River to the northern Adirondacks and northern Lake \nChamplain. A cold front is expected to cross the region this \nmorning, bringing additional showers and thunderstorms. Probability \nof precipitation in a given location is around 50-80% in the western \nAdirondacks, St. Lawrence Valley, and far northern Vermont. \nProbability of precipitation is lowest in the souther Champlain and \nConnecticut River valleys, around 5-30%. Additional anticipated \nrainfall amounts range around 0.10-0.30 inches through the end of \ntoday, though higher precip amounts and gusty winds could occur in \nany thunderstorms. Outside of storms, southwesterly winds are also \nanticipated throughout the day today with gusts as high as 20-30 \nknots likely. The cold front should keep temperatures from reaching \ntoo high today, particularly in northern New York, where highs will \nbe in the 60s west of the Champlain Valley. In the Champlain Valley \nand remainder of Vermont, temperatures are forecast in the mid and \nupper 70s, warmest in the Connecticut River Valley where the cold \nfront will visit last. Depending on the speed of the cold front, \ntemperatures could end up being a degree or two warmer or colder.\nWednesday night through Friday night will be dry with high pressure \nin control. Humidity levels will be comfortable with highs in the \n70s to lower 80s Thursday and upper 70s to mid 80s Friday. Lows will \nfall into the 40s and 50s each night, with radiational fog potential \nin the typical valley locations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure starts to shift to the east by the second \nhalf of the weekend, leading to increasing S/SW flow. The result \nwill be warming temperatures, with highs to be mainly in the \nmid/upper 80s both Saturday and Sunday. Humidity doesn't look too \nbad  as dewpoints are expected to remain the upper 50s to mid 60s. \nOur next chance of precipitation arrives late Sunday into Monday and \nlikely persists into mid week as upper troughiness sets up across \neastern Canada and the northeastern CONUS. Shortwaves rotating \naround the upper trough will bring periods of showers and \nthunderstorms. These sorts of features are typically difficult to \nnail down in timing and strength this far out, and indeed, model \nsolutions differ quite a bit from Sunday night onward. Have stayed \nwith the NBM/WPC forecast for now given the uncertainty, which \nresults in chances of showers/thunderstorms each day. Temperatures \nlook to remain seasonable through the period.",
